<p>The World Health Organization declared the Ebola outbreak in the Democratic Republic of Congo and Uganda a public health emergency of international concern, saying the event requires international coordination but does not meet the criteria for a pandemic emergency. The outbreak is caused by the Bundibugyo ebolavirus, a rarer Ebola species for which WHO said there are no approved virus-specific vaccines or therapeutics. WHO said authorities had reported eight laboratory-confirmed cases, 246 suspected cases and 80 suspected deaths in DRC’s Ituri province as of May 16. Other international reports put suspected cases above 300 and deaths at 88, while WHO warned the outbreak could be larger than currently detected and reported. WHO cited confirmed spread into Uganda, uncertainty over the outbreak’s true scale and risks to countries sharing land borders with DRC. Uganda reported two laboratory-confirmed cases, one of them fatal, in its capital, Kampala, among people who had traveled from DRC; other reports said cases had also been reported in Kinshasa.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/vq9mgg8bUlCam9czBTwG9x2d_5r4PqSgM5pC0r52w1X0mn-RY3sRRDrYJCckBeSPlfwGGtYlPvMcRBNSyBdSWfUdsaxcs-DklCtN31vcuZFD' alt='A staff member of the CBCA Virunga Hospital checks a visitor’s temperature in Goma, Democratic Republic of Congo, on May 17, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Affected zones: Authorities reported suspected DRC cases across at least three Ituri health zones: Bunia, Rwampara and Mongbwalu.</li><li>Urban concern: Laboratory testing confirmed a case in Goma, a major eastern DRC city controlled by the Rwanda-backed M23 militia.</li><li>Health-worker concern: ICIR reported infections among health workers in the outbreak.</li><li>Warning signs: WHO pointed to clusters of unexplained community deaths and a high positivity rate in initial samples as signals that surveillance may be missing cases.</li></ul><blockquote>The event requires international coordination and cooperation to understand the extent of the outbreak, to coordinate surveillance, prevention and response efforts, to scale up and strengthen operations and ensure ability to implement control measures - World Health Organization</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>World Health Organization: WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus made the PHEIC determination after consulting DRC and Uganda. WHO thanked both governments for their leadership and cooperation under the International Health Regulations. (<a href='https://www.who.int/news/item/17-05-2026-epidemic-of-ebola-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-uganda-determined-a-public-health-emergency-of-international-concern'>WHO</a>)</li><li>Nigeria Centre for Disease Control and Prevention: NCDC said Nigeria had no confirmed Ebola cases and had intensified surveillance at ports of entry after the WHO declaration. (<a href='https://www.bellanaija.com/2026/05/who-declares-ebola-outbreak-drc-uganda-global-health-emergency-2026/'>BellaNaija</a>)</li><li>Vietnam Ministry of Health: Vietnam’s health ministry said it had not recorded global spread of the outbreak and had increased border health surveillance, quarantine measures and advice for people returning from affected areas. (<a href='https://nhandan.vn/ebola-o-congo-uganda-thanh-tinh-trang-khan-cap-quoc-te-bo-y-te-phat-khuyen-cao-post962841.html'>Nhan Dan</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/17/world/africa/ebola-congo-uganda-who-public-health-emergency.html'>W.H.O. Declares Ebola Outbreak a Global Health Emergency</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/who-declares-ebola-outbreak-in-congo-uganda-a-global-public-health-emergency/article70989497.ece'>WHO declares international emergency as Ebola outbreak kills more than 80 in DR Congo</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/aa55ba2b-8476-4718-8d28-5f60df6ff19c'>WHO declares global health e